StrokeMaster has two native parts, each built with its host's SDK: the InDesign
plugins, built with the Adobe InDesign SDK, and the Illustrator plugin, built
with the Adobe Illustrator SDK. Each gets the same exception, for the SDK it is
built with.

Why this is here
----------------

A plugin for either application cannot be built without that application's
SDK, and the resulting binary is not merely linked against it: Adobe ships
part of the SDK as *source*, and every plugin compiles that source into itself.

- Both InDesign plugins, the engine (InDesignStrokeEngine.pln) and the panel
  (StrokeMasterUI.pln), compile these three of Adobe's files:

      SDKPlugInEntrypoint.cpp  PlugInStatics.cpp  VCPlugInHeaders.cpp

  and link the SDK's libraries.

- The Illustrator plugin (StrokeMasterRegionProbe.aip, a working name)
  compiles Adobe's

      IAIUnicodeString.cpp  (with IAIUnicodeString.inl)

  and the inline code of the SDK's headers, and embeds a plugin resource
  (PiPL) generated by the SDK's create_pipl.py.

Each of those files carries Adobe's copyright and a license permitting use only
"in accordance with the terms of the Adobe license agreement accompanying it."
That is a restriction GPL section 10 forbids anyone from imposing on a
recipient, and section 5(c) requires the whole combined work to be licensed
under the GPL, which no one but Adobe is in a position to do for Adobe's code.

So the source in this repository is unambiguously GPL: it contains no Adobe
code, and both SDKs are referenced by path rather than vendored. The *compiled
plugins* would not be distributable under a bare GPL, because the license they
purported to grant would cover code the copyright holder has no authority to
relicense. Section 7 exists for exactly this situation, and this file uses it.

